From: Paolo Abeni <pabeni@redhat.com>

The MPTCP protocol uses a significantly different CB layout WRT TCP, as it
includes different information and uses 64 bits for the sequence numbers.

As the msk-level rcvbuf buffer size is limited by the core socket code to
INT_MAX, and after validating the incoming skb vs the current receive
window, we can safely use 32 bits for MPTCP-level sequence numbers. This
allows updating the MPTCP CB layout so that key fields with corresponding
TCP-level data (map_seq/seq, end_seq/end_seq, flags/tcp_flags) use the
same offsets inside the CB itself.

The CB layout changes map_seq and end_seq from u64 to u32, adds a new
map_seq64 field to track the full 64-bit DSN, and aligns the existing flags
field with TCP's tcp_flags offset. The 32-bit sequence numbers are safe
because all SKBs in the receive queue and OFO queue have been validated
against the receive window (max INT_MAX), so their sequence numbers are
guaranteed to be within 2^31 of the current ack_seq or copied_seq. This
ensures that 32-bit arithmetic (comparisons and subtractions) produces
correct results even when 64-bit values cross the 2^32 boundary, as the
relative distance remains small.

Add a build time check to ensure the CB layout matches TCP's layout for the
three shared fields: map_seq, end_seq, and flags.

map_seq64 is computed as mptcp_subflow_get_mapped_dsn() in mptcp_init_skb()
minus offset, and map_seq is the lower 32 bits.

The receive path now uses 32-bit before()/after() helpers for most sequence
comparisons, while map_seq64 is used for window validation and coalescing
checks. __mptcp_try_coalesce() gains a map_seq64 contiguity check to ensure
skbs are truly adjacent in the 64-bit DSN space.

